    id: 'science-5-note-breathingsystem',
    subjectId: 'science',
    grade: 5,
    topic: 'The Human Breathing System',
    title: 'The Human Breathing System Study Note',
    body: "Hello there, young scientist! Let's dive into the amazing world of the human breathing system. The human breathing system is made up of several important parts, including the nose, trachea, lungs, and diaphragm, each playing a unique role in helping us breathe. The nose is where it all starts, as we inhale air through our nostrils, and the trachea, or windpipe, carries the air down into our lungs, where oxygen is absorbed into the bloodstream. The diaphragm, a special muscle, helps us breathe in and out by moving up and down, kind of like a big, muscular balloon. For example, imagine you're playing soccer at the Kenyatta Stadium in Nairobi - you need to breathe in oxygen to give you energy to run around, and that's where your lungs come in, exchanging the oxygen from the air you breathe for the carbon dioxide your body doesn't need. To keep our breathing system healthy, we need to be careful about the air we breathe, avoiding pollutants and germs that can cause common conditions like colds, coughs, and even COVID-19, and taking steps to prevent them, like washing our hands regularly and wearing masks when necessary.",
