    body: "Hello there, young learner! Today, we're going to explore the important theme of 'Child Labour'. Child labour refers to work done by children at a very young age, often in difficult conditions and for long hours, which can be harmful to their health and well-being. Labour, in this context, means any kind of work that children are forced to do, such as working in factories, farms, or even as domestic servants in homes. For example, imagine a young boy named Kipenzi who is only 12 years old and has to work in a tea farm in Kericho for many hours a day, earning very low wages, which is a clear case of child labour. On the other hand, 'Child' refers to a young human being who is still growing and needs care and protection, like the children living in an orphanage in Nairobi, who should be in school learning and playing, not working in difficult conditions. It's essential to understand that children, especially those who are underage or teenagers, should not be engaged in any form of labour that interferes with their education or harms their health. As we learn more about this theme, we'll discover how child labour affects many children in Kenya and around the world, and what we can do to help prevent it.",
